---
import MotionPage from '@/components/MotionPage.astro';
import Code from '@/components/Code.astro';
import MotionUsage from '@/components/motion/MotionUsage.astro';

const layoutSnippet = `// The card has a layout motion, such as data-motion="reflow". A layout motion does not observe an inline style,
// so setting the width moves nothing by itself.
const card = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('#card')!;
card.style.width = '50%';

// A new data-motion-state calls play(): the layout is measured now, and the card moves from its old box to its new one.
// In a framework, pass the value that changes as state: <Motion name="reflow" state={wide} style={{ width }}>.
card.dataset.motionState = 'half';`;
---
<MotionPage
  slug="troubleshooting"
  title="Motion: troubleshooting"
  description="What to check when a motion of @udixio/motion does not play, is ignored on a component, erases a style, stretches text, loses focus, keeps running, takes the spacing away or replays on hydration: each symptom with its cause and its fix."
>
  <p>
    Find your symptom, then check its causes in order. Each cause says what the runtime does, and what to change. To see what
    the runtime holds on a page, call <code>inspect()</code> from <code>@udixio/motion/scope</code>: it lists the
    reduced-motion policy and its source, each binding with its element, kind, name and state, and the defined motions.
  </p>

  <h2 id="nothing-animates">Nothing animates</h2>

  <h3>The content was there before the runtime</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An entry plays on a change that happens after the element is bound. In HTML, an element that is on
    the page when <code>connectMotion()</code> runs is already in place, so it plays no entry, and a reveal that is in view
    when it is bound is left as it is. React treats hydrated server content as in place, and Angular marks the elements it
    renders on the server <code>data-motion-presence="present"</code>, which binds them without an entry.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Make the change after binding: insert the element, or remove its <code>hidden</code>, or turn
    <code>present</code> to true. To play an entry as the page opens, bind the element yourself:
    <code>motion(element, 'slide')</code> plays the entry of an element that carries no <code>data-motion</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>A class hides the element</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The runtime follows the <code>hidden</code> attribute in HTML, and <code>present</code> or the
    framework's own condition in the adapters. It does not read your classes. A class that sets <code>display: none</code>,
    such as Tailwind's <code>hidden</code> utility, hides the element at once, before any exit; an element that a class
    keeps at <code>display: none</code> plays its entry where nobody can see it.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Toggle the attribute, not a class: <code>element.hidden = true</code>. During the exit the runtime
    keeps the element displayed, then lets <code>hidden</code> take over. Check that no rule of yours gives the element a
    <code>display</code> that beats <code>[hidden]</code>; Tailwind's base styles already make <code>[hidden]</code> win.
  </p>

  <h3>Motion is turned off or reduced</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>--udx-motion: none</code> on the element or an ancestor (the Tailwind utility
    <code>motion-none</code>) applies the end state at once. Under reduced motion, from the system setting or from
    <code>&lt;html data-motion-policy="reduce"&gt;</code>, an entry and an exit become a short fade, an emphasis applies its
    end frame, a reveal shows at once, a layout takes its new place at once and a loop does not play.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Read <code>getComputedStyle(element).getPropertyValue('--udx-motion')</code>, and
    <code>motionPolicy.reduced</code> from <code>@udixio/motion/policy</code>; <code>inspect()</code> names the source of the
    policy. Test with reduced motion on too: it is a setting of your visitors, not a defect. See
    <a href="/motion/accessibility">Reduced motion</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>The name is not defined yet</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An unknown name leaves the element untouched. <code>connectMotion()</code> and the adapters bind
    the element as soon as the name is defined, but a motion bound that late is settled: it skips the entry it missed, and only
    the next change plays.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Call <code>defineMotion</code> in a module that runs before the elements that use it render, such as
    your app's main module, not in a component that renders later. In development, React, Angular and Svelte log an error that names
    the missing motion and lists the defined ones, and the element waits for it; <code>connectMotion(&#123; development: true &#125;)</code>
    does the same in HTML.
  </p>

  <h3>The presets are not imported</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>fade</code>, <code>slide</code>, <code>scale</code>, <code>reflow</code> and the other
    presets are defined by the import path <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code>. Without it, their names are unknown, as above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Import it once, next to <code>connectMotion()</code> or in your app's main module:
    <code>import '@udixio/motion/presets';</code>. Without a bundler, map <code>@udixio/motion/presets</code> in your import
    map too. See <a href="/motion/presets">Presets and verbs</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ignored-on-component">Your motion is ignored on a component</h2>
  <p>
    A motion on a Udixio component, through <code>&lt;Motion as=&#123;Component&#125;&gt;</code> in React,
    <code>udxMotion</code> on its tag in Angular or <code>&#123;&#64;attach motion()&#125;</code> in Svelte, moves the
    component's <a href="/motion/glossary#term-box">box</a>, its root element. When nothing moves, development says why on
    the console, once per element, if the <a href="#development-checks">development checks</a> are on.
  </p>

  <h3>The component already holds that kind</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A component that animates one of its elements holds that
    <a href="/motion/glossary#term-reserved-kind">kind</a> there: a Snackbar holds the presence of its panel, a SideSheet the
    presence and the layout of its panel, a NavigationRail the layout of its rail. A motion of that kind on the same element
    is ignored, and the console names the owner:
  </p>
  <p><code>[@udixio/motion] "fade" ignored: Snackbar already holds a presence on this element, for its own animation: an element carries one motion of each kind.</code></p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Time the component's own animation with its <code>motion</code> prop, where it has one (a
    Snackbar, a SideSheet, a Tooltip, a NavigationRail), such as
    <code>motion=&#123;&#123; timing: 'spring.slow.spatial' &#125;&#125;</code> on a SideSheet. A motion of another kind
    still plays: a <code>press</code> on a Snackbar works. Each component's page says which kinds it holds, and
    <a href="/motion/choosing-an-api#element-or-component">Choosing an API</a> lists them all.
  </p>

  <h3>The component has no box to give</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> Its surface is in a portal or the top layer, away from where you write it: a Tooltip, an
    AnchorPositioner, a ContextMenu, a modal SideSheet in React; <code>udx-anchor-positioner</code>,
    <code>udx-context-menu</code> and a modal <code>udx-side-sheet</code> in Angular; a ContextMenu in Svelte. It carries
    <code>data-motion-box="none"</code>, and the motion binds nothing rather than moving its anchor. The console names the
    element, here the element that React's <code>&lt;Motion as=&#123;Tooltip&#125;&gt;</code> renders:
  </p>
  <p><code>[@udixio/motion] "press" ignored: &lt;span&gt; has no box to give (data-motion-box="none"): its component shows its surface elsewhere (a portal, the top layer). Animate the component with its own motion prop.</code></p>
  <p>
    In Angular the element is the host, <code>&lt;udx-context-menu…&gt;</code> with its first classes; in Svelte, the root
    of a ContextMenu, <code>&lt;div&gt;</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Time the component with its own <code>motion</code> prop where it has one: a Tooltip
    (<code>udxTooltipMotion</code> in Angular) or a SideSheet. A ContextMenu and an AnchorPositioner have none: put the
    motion on the element you give them, such as the trigger, or on the content inside them.
  </p>

  <h3>A wrapper is display: contents from a stylesheet only</h3>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The runtime reads the signals of an element with no box without the computed style: its own
    <code>style="display: contents"</code>, <code>data-motion-box="child"</code>, or the <code>boxless</code> option. A
    wrapper that a class or a stylesheet makes <code>display: contents</code> is not declared, so the motion stays on it, and
    a wrapper with no box has nothing to move. The console says so:
  </p>
  <p><code>[@udixio/motion] &lt;div.toast-host&gt; has no box (display: contents): declare it so its motion goes to its box (data-motion-box="child" on it, or the boxless option).</code></p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Add <code>data-motion-box="child"</code> to the wrapper. If it has several element children, mark
    the one that moves with <code>data-motion-box</code>: otherwise its first element child moves, and the console says
    <code>has no box (display: contents) and several children: mark the box with data-motion-box</code>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="inline-style">Your inline style is ignored or erased</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> While a presence, an emphasis or a reveal plays, the runtime writes <code>opacity</code>,
    <code>translate</code>, <code>scale</code> and <code>rotate</code> inline on the element; it never writes
    <code>transform</code>. At rest, it removes the ones that are back at their neutral value (opacity 1, no translation,
    scale 1, no rotation), so your stylesheet applies again. A value that is not neutral stays inline, such as the opacity 0
    of an exit until <code>hidden</code> takes over. So an inline style you set on one of these properties is overwritten
    while the element is bound, and removed at rest.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Set these properties with a class (<code>opacity-80</code>, <code>scale-105</code>,
    <code>hover:scale-105</code>): a class applies at rest, between motions. Or move the style to a wrapper element that has
    no motion. A gesture writes <code>translate</code> as well, so the same holds for a dragged element.
  </p>

  <h2 id="layout-not-observed">A layout move does not play after a style change</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A layout motion watches its scope, the closest ancestor with <code>data-motion-scope</code> or else
    the element's parent: a class or <code>data-*</code> change, a child added, removed or reordered, or <code>hidden</code>
    changing. An inline <code>style</code> change is not observed, nor is a change outside the scope. The layout engine also
    loads the first time a layout motion is bound, and a change made before it has loaded is not animated.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Change a class or a <code>data-*</code> attribute instead of the inline style. When you must write
    the style, give the element the value that changes as its state (<code>data-motion-state</code>, <code>state</code>,
    <code>[udxMotionState]</code>): each new value calls <code>play()</code>, which compares the layout once the style is
    applied. From a control, <code>play('layout')</code> does the same. Widen the scope with
    <code>data-motion-scope</code> on an ancestor that holds the change. Call <code>await loadLayoutEngine()</code> to load the
    engine ahead of the first change.
  </p>
  <Code data-code={layoutSnippet} data-language="ts" />

  <h2 id="stretched-text">Text is stretched during a layout move</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A layout move scales the element from its old size to its new one. The elements inside it keep
    their natural size, up to three levels deep and 24 elements, but text placed directly in the moving element has no element
    of its own to correct, so it is stretched with the box.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Wrap the text in an element. Keep <code>transform-origin</code> at the centre of the moving element,
    where the move scales it.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-troubleshooting-stretched" live={false} />

  <h2 id="presence-child">A child of &lt;Presence&gt; leaves without its exit</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> React's <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> keeps a child that left rendered until every
    <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> inside it reports that its exit has ended. A motion run by the <code>useMotion</code> hook does
    not report to the Presence, so a child that holds only a hook leaves at once. A child with no motion leaves at once too.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Render the animated element with <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>; <code>as</code> chooses its tag. Give
    each child of the Presence a <code>key</code>: it is how the Presence knows which child left. Keep <code>useMotion</code>
    for an element outside a Presence, which its <code>mounted</code> flag keeps rendered while it leaves.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-troubleshooting-presence-child" live={false} />

  <h2 id="element-keeps-its-place">An element that left still takes its place</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>setPresent(false)</code> on a <code>motion()</code> binding plays the exit, then leaves the
    element in the first frame of its motion: transparent for the presets, and <code>inert</code>. It keeps its box in the
    layout, and the server HTML shows it. Only <code>hidden</code> takes an element out of the layout.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Set <code>hidden</code> on the element once <code>setPresent(false)</code> has resolved to
    <code>'finished'</code>. In the frameworks, <code>present</code> hides the element once its exit has played: give it
    to <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code>, to <code>[udxMotionPresent]</code> in Angular, or to the option of the Svelte attachment.
    With <code>useMotion</code>, also set <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> on the element. For content that leaves the page,
    let the framework remove it: <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> in React, <code>&#64;if</code> in Angular, or
    <code>&#123;#if&#125;</code> with <code>transition:presence</code> in Svelte. For content that stays in the DOM, such as an
    accordion's answers, see the <a href="/motion/recipes#accordion">accordion recipe</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="focus-lost">Focus is lost when an element leaves</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An exit makes the element <code>inert</code>, and the browser then drops the focus that was inside
    it. Without a target, the runtime keeps the element focusable until focus leaves it or the exit ends, and a development
    build warns once per element.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Name where focus goes, usually the control that opened the element (WCAG 2.4.3). The runtime moves
    focus there before it sets <code>inert</code>; a target that cannot take focus gets <code>tabindex="-1"</code> for that
    move. React: <code>restoreFocus</code> on <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> or in <code>useMotion</code>. Angular:
    <code>[udxMotionRestoreFocus]</code>. Svelte: <code>restoreFocus</code> in the options of <code>motion</code>. HTML:
    <code>data-motion-restore-focus="#opener"</code>. Pass a function when the target renders after the element binds.
  </p>
  <MotionUsage name="motion-presence-focus" live={false} />

  <h2 id="loop-off-screen">A loop or an autoplay keeps running off-screen</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> A <code>loop</code> motion pauses by itself while its element is out of view or its tab is hidden,
    and does not play under reduced motion. Something that keeps running is not a loop of <code>@udixio/motion</code>: a CSS
    animation, a <code>setInterval</code> that advances a carousel, an <code>animateStyle</code> started by a timer.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Define the motion with the verb <code>loop</code>, or run your own timer only while the element is
    active: <code>watchActive(element, onChange)</code> from <code>@udixio/motion/active</code> tells you when it is in view, in
    a shown tab, and with motion not reduced. An element that moves inside a box that clips it, such as a carousel track with
    <code>overflow: hidden</code>, is in view while that box is: a loop finds that box by itself, and
    <code>watchActive(viewedBox(element), onChange)</code> does the same for your timer. Anything that moves for more than five
    seconds also needs a visible pause control (WCAG 2.2.2): the <code>paused</code> option, or
    <code>data-motion-paused</code> in HTML. See <a href="/motion/loop">Loop, and pause it</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="page-jumps">The page jumps when content enters</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> An entry animates the opacity and the transform of the element, which do not move the layout. The
    element takes its full place the moment it is inserted or shown, and the content below it moves at once; when an exit
    ends, its place is freed at once.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Reserve the space when you know the size: a minimum height on the container, an aspect ratio on
    media. Take overlays out of the flow: a dialog, a menu or a toast over the page is <code>fixed</code> or
    <code>absolute</code>. Otherwise give the neighbours <code>reflow</code>, so that they glide to their new place; in a list,
    the exit option <code>out-of-flow</code>, with a positioned parent, lets them close the gap while the item fades. See
    <a href="/motion/lists">Make a list appear</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spacing-around-component">Spacing disappears around &lt;Motion as=&#123;Component&#125;&gt;</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>&lt;Motion as=&#123;Component&#125;&gt;</code> creates no box, but it puts an element of its
    own between your parent and the component; the host of every Udixio Angular component does the same, motion or not.
    Utilities that style the parent's direct children, or compare siblings, then reach that element, which has no box,
    and not the component: <code>space-x-*</code>, <code>space-y-*</code>, <code>divide-*</code>,
    <code>[&amp;&gt;*]:…</code>, and <code>first:</code>, <code>last:</code> or <code>peer-*</code> on the component. A
    Svelte attachment adds no element, so this does not happen there.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Space the components with <code>gap</code> on a flex or grid parent:
    <code>&lt;div className="flex gap-4"&gt;</code>. An element with no box takes no place in the layout, so the component's
    root is the flex or grid item, and the gap applies to it. Put a border or a margin on the component itself, through its
    <code>className</code> (<code>class</code> in Angular), rather than from the parent.
  </p>

  <h2 id="development-checks">The development checks are missing in your bundler's dev build</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The package has two builds. The default build runs the development checks (the validation of the
    specs, and the warnings) only where <code>process.env.NODE_ENV</code> is not <code>production</code>; a browser has no
    <code>process</code>, so there they are off, as in production. The development build always runs them, and the
    <code>development</code> export condition selects it. Vite's dev server, webpack in development mode and
    <code>node --conditions=development</code> use that condition; a bundler that is not set up for it loads the default build.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> Add the <code>development</code> condition to your bundler's resolve conditions for development
    builds only, for example <code>--conditions=development</code> with esbuild. The runtime messages of
    <code>connectMotion()</code>, such as an unknown name, need <code>connectMotion(&#123; development: true &#125;)</code>
    in any build. The framework adapters follow the same checks: they report the same messages wherever the checks are on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hydration">Server-rendered content flashes or replays its entry on hydration</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> The server cannot play a motion, and an option such as <code>present</code> acts in the browser
    only. What the runtime does with server HTML depends on the framework:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>React treats hydrated content as already in place: no entry. The exits of <code>&lt;Presence&gt;</code> exist in the browser only; the server renders the current children.</li>
    <li>Angular marks the present elements it renders on the server <code>data-motion-presence="present"</code>, and the browser binds them without an entry.</li>
    <li>Svelte cannot run an attachment on the server, so the element is not marked, and its entry replays on hydration.</li>
    <li>In HTML, an element on the page when <code>connectMotion()</code> runs plays no entry.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> In Svelte, pass <code>settled: true</code> in the options of <code>motion</code> for content the
    server renders: <code>&#123;@attach motion('slide', () =&gt; (&#123; settled: true &#125;))&#125;</code>. For content that
    starts closed, send it closed: give it the <code>hidden</code> attribute in the server HTML, so that it never shows before
    the browser binds it. In Svelte, an element with <code>present</code> needs <code>hidden=&#123;!open&#125;</code> in its markup too,
    since the server runs no attachment.
  </p>

  <h2 id="react-hydration-warning">React warns that the server HTML did not match</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Cause.</strong> <code>connectMotion()</code> binds the <code>data-motion</code> elements as soon as it runs, which
    can be before React hydrates them: it writes <code>data-motion-presence</code>, <code>inert</code> or an inline
    <code>opacity</code> on them. React's development build compares these attributes with its first render and reports a
    hydration mismatch.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Fix.</strong> In a React tree rendered on the server, bind motions with <code>&lt;Motion&gt;</code> or
    <code>useMotion</code>: they bind once React has hydrated. Keep <code>data-motion</code> for markup that React does not
    render. The <a href="/motion/recipes#accordion">accordion recipe</a> shows an answer kept in the DOM with
    <code>&lt;Motion present&gt;</code>.
  </p>
</MotionPage>
